# Line endings.
#
# This repository was authored on a Unix-like system and every text file uses LF.
# Both developers now work on Windows. Without this file, Git's Windows defaults can
# rewrite line endings on checkout, which makes a one-line edit appear in review as a
# whole-file rewrite and hides the actual change.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: detect text files, store them with LF, and check them out
# with LF on every platform. Modern Windows editors, including VS Code, handle LF
# correctly. The bootstrap script also sets core.autocrlf=false globally; this file
# makes the repository correct regardless of anyone's global configuration.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF or some shells mis-parse them.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never convert, never diff as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.m4a   binary
*.opus  binary
*.ogg   binary
*.wav   binary
*.pdf   binary

# Generated files. Collapsing them in pull request diffs keeps a dependency bump from
# burying a real change under thousands of lines. They are still versioned and still
# reviewed — `linguist-generated` only affects the default diff view.
package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true -diff
public/assets/**   linguist-generated=true -diff
